--TEST--
All four rendering spellings agree that a `Core`-owned class the spec gives no `toString` is `E0710` where it is written, and none of them fires on one that has it — `rule:classes/stringable`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:classes/stringable` makes `Stringable` the one way an object renders. A `Core`
// class declares no interfaces to prove it against — its members are
// `nvs_stdlib::registry` rows backed by native symbols — so that registry
// is the one home for which `Core` classes render, and one it gives no
// `toString` is refused where it is written rather than below it.
class Render {
    public static function echoed(Core\Time\Instant $at): void {
        echo $at;
    }

    public static function interpolated(Core\Time\Instant $at): string {
        return "at $at";
    }

    public static function concatenated(Core\Time\Instant $at): string {
        return "at " . $at;
    }

    public static function converted(Core\Time\Instant $at): string {
        return $at as string;
    }
}

// The control, and the reason this case asserts agreement rather than four
// separate refusals: `Core\Uuid` is one the spec *does* give a `toString`,
// so none of the four fires on it and the expectation below carries no
// diagnostic for this line.
class Ok {
    public static function converted(Core\Uuid $id): string {
        return $id as string;
    }
}
